---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { projects, STATUS_CRITERIA, STATUS_REVIEWED, ORG } from '../data/projects.js';
import { loadGithub, loadManifest, shortSha } from '../lib/synced.js';

const github = await loadGithub();
const manifest = await loadManifest();
---

<Base
  title="StellarBrief"
  description="Open-source tools for understanding, testing and communicating Stellar protocol upgrades: preflight checks for Soroban contracts, local validator upgrade drills, and plain-language advisory briefs."
  path="/"
>
  <h1>Open-source tooling for Stellar protocol upgrades</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Three small tools for the people affected by a Stellar upgrade: contract developers, validator
    operators, and anyone who has to explain an advisory to a team. The GitHub repositories are the
    product. This site shows what they actually do, using real recorded runs.
  </p>

  <div class="grid">
    {
      projects.map((p) => {
        const data = github.repos[p.repo];
        return (
          <section class="card" aria-labelledby={`card-${p.slug}`}>
            <h2 id={`card-${p.slug}`} style="font-size:1.25rem;margin:0">{p.name}</h2>
            <p>
              <span class="badge status" title={STATUS_CRITERIA[p.status]}>{p.status}</span>
              {data?.latestRelease && <span class="small"> &middot; latest release {data.latestRelease.tag}</span>}
            </p>
            <p><strong>{p.tagline}</strong></p>
            <p>{p.summary}</p>
            <p class="small">For: {p.audience}.{p.needsDocker && ' Needs Docker to run.'}</p>
            <p class="links">
              <a href={`/projects/${p.slug}/`}>About</a>
              <a href={p.playground}>See it work</a>
              <a href={`https://github.com/${ORG}/${p.repo}`}>GitHub</a>
            </p>
          </section>
        );
      })
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Why these exist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A protocol upgrade can change what a contract costs or whether it runs. Developers get a short window
      to check, and no easy way to compare two protocol versions side by side.
    </li>
    <li>
      Validator operators need to know how an upgrade vote behaves before it matters, including when one
      validator misses it. That is hard to rehearse without a real network.
    </li>
    <li>
      Advisories are written for node operators. Everyone downstream has to translate them under time
      pressure, and a summary is only useful if you can check it against the source.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the tools relate</h2>
  <p>
    They share no code and no data. They serve different people in the same upgrade cycle.
    <span class="badge kind-example">Example</span> A simplified timeline:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>An announcement is published.</strong> Advisory Brief helps someone read it and decide who needs to act.</li>
    <li><strong>Before the vote:</strong> contract developers use Upgrade Preflight to compare their contracts across the two protocol versions.</li>
    <li><strong>Before the vote:</strong> validator operators use Upgrade Drill to rehearse how the vote behaves on a small local network.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Honest status</h2>
  <p>
    All three are <strong>Experimental</strong> (reviewed {STATUS_REVIEWED}): {STATUS_CRITERIA.Experimental} Each
    project page explains its own limits.
  </p>

  <p class="small">
    This page reflects commits
    {
      Object.entries(manifest.repos).map(([repo, m], i) => (
        <>{i > 0 ? ', ' : ' '}<code>{repo}@{shortSha(m.sha)}</code></>
      ))
    }. GitHub data was fetched at build time on {github.fetchedAt.slice(0, 10)}, so it is a recording, not live.
  </p>
</Base>
